#!/bin/bash
# 686 (Fri 20 Nov 2026, CEO chat order pasted by Peter 08:44 - replaces the earlier orders on method). ONE TEST, ONE LEAGUE TABLE.
# Starts one builder session on the server with the brief below. It turns the recorded-prices engine into a single test that every
# way of earning goes through, builds the league table, loads the first ways of earning from all three sources, moves passing rows to
# forward paper, and stops. Paper only. No orders. Nothing deleted. The hourly progress file and the Friday email then read the table.
B=/root/pe-builder; R=/root/pe-queue/reports/builder_686.md
cat > $B/BRIEF_league.md <<'BRIEF'
You are the Builder on Peter Edwards' trading server, session 2. You are started once. Do the job, leave ordinary background processes running, write your handover, stop. No idle loops; long runs go to the background with nohup + nice.

FIRST read /root/pe-queue/reports/builder_handover.md (session 1) and the code in /root/pe-sports/grid/ (engine.py, test_known.py, settle.py, grid_recorded.py). Reuse it. Do not rebuild what works.

PETER'S INSTRUCTION (20 Nov 2026, replaces all earlier orders on method):
GOAL: profit after all costs, towards £10,000 a month. No method is preferred. None is ruled out.
1. Ideas come from three places, all open: (a) how proven profitable exchange traders actually earn - study them and copy what works; (b) what our own data shows; (c) anything else, including things nobody else does.
2. ONE TEST FOR EVERYTHING: real prices on offer, after the gap between back and lay and after commission, counting only orders that would really have been filled. Taking prices, offering prices, trading in and out, across markets, across exchanges: every way of earning enters the same test.
3. ONE LEAGUE TABLE ranked by £ a month at the stake the market takes. Each row: the way of earning in one plain sentence, where the idea came from (a, b or c), bets or orders, £ kept per £100, both halves, £ a month.
4. Keep the crumbs: anything that passes goes to paper, however small. Many small ones added together are the business.
5. Never narrow this to one method. Never report history at last traded prices as money.
6. Every Friday: the league table, how many ways of earning were tested that week, how many new ones were added from each of the three sources.
Proof gate unchanged (100 settled forward paper bets, profit after commission overall and in each half, in line with the test, then Peter's yes). No real money.

WHAT TO BUILD (all in /root/pe-sports/grid/):
A. THE ONE TEST. A function every way of earning calls: it is given a rule (which markets, when to enter, which side, taking or offering, when and how to exit) and returns, on the recorded real-price data (stream_ticks, ip_tape, book_snap, xx_tape and their archive days), the list of orders that WOULD REALLY HAVE FILLED and their profit. Rules of the test, fixed for every row: take = fill at the best price on offer, only up to the money shown there, minimum £2 on offer; offer = an order rested at a price fills only when a later trade goes THROUGH that price (a trade AT the price is not a fill), and only for money that actually traded; stake = what the market takes, capped at £50 a bet and £50 lay liability; 2% commission on net market winnings; an exit that cannot fill is held to the result; a quote must be fresh; halves by date; a row that has never lost is flagged, not ranked. Extend test_known.py with a hand-worked example for each of: a take-and-hold, a take in-and-out, an offer that fills, an offer that is only touched (must NOT fill), an exit that cannot fill, a two-market trade, a two-exchange trade. All must pass before any full run.
B. THE REGISTRY: table earn_ways(id, sentence, source CHAR(1) in a/b/c, origin TEXT (who or what it came from), rule JSONB, added_at, status). One row per way of earning, written as ONE PLAIN SENTENCE a non-trader can read, no abbreviations, no code names.
C. THE LEAGUE TABLE: table league(way_id, run_at, orders, kept_per_100, half1, half2, gbp_per_month, days_of_data, losers, verdict) and the file /root/pe-queue/reports/league_table.md, ranked by £ a month, every way tested shown (passing first), with a header line: ways tested, ways passing (300 or more filled orders and profit in both halves), ways on forward paper, and this week's count of new ways by source a / b / c. Plain English only.
D. FIRST LOAD OF WAYS, from all three sources. Do not stop at this list; add every sensible variant (sport, market, timing, price band, depth) as its own row. Aim for hundreds of rows in this session and make adding a row a one-line job.
 Source (a), how proven exchange traders are documented to earn - turn each into mechanical rules and test: pre-race scalping in the last 10 minutes on UK and Irish horse racing (offer on both sides a tick apart, out before the off); pre-race swing trading with the weight of money (follow the side with more money waiting); backing a steamer early and laying it shorter, and the reverse for drifters; in-running racing 'back to lay' on front runners and 'lay to back' on hold-up horses; lay the draw in football and close after a goal; lay the leader after an early goal; back the favourite at half time when level; over/under time decay (lay overs and close as the clock runs); correct-score cover trades; tennis: lay the server at break point prices, back the player who lost the first set, trade the swing after a break; cricket: lay the favourite at short prices in limited overs; market making in thin markets generally (rest both sides, earn the gap); trading the reopening after a suspension (goal, wicket, break).
 Source (b), our own data: every slice of the recorded-prices grid (grid_agg2) and the strongest PRE-START slices of the history grid (grid_agg, source 'history') enter as rows and are tested here at real prices. History numbers are ideas only and never appear as £.
 Source (c), anything else: the same event priced differently on Betfair, Smarkets and Matchbook (take both sides when they cross after commission; offer on the slow exchange at the fast one's price); markets on one match that must agree (match odds against correct score, over/under against correct score, half time against full time, double chance against match odds) - trade the one out of line; prices that lag an event on another market of the same match; quoting in thin markets at a price built from the deep ones; time-of-day and day-of-week effects in liquidity; anything you find in the data that fits no category.
E. FORWARD PAPER: any way that passes is marked 'paper' and from that moment its fills on NEW days only are tracked separately (table paper_forward) towards the proof gate of 100 settled orders. Write the paper rows into the format the existing scoreboard can read, or produce /root/pe-queue/reports/paper_forward.md with one line per way: orders settled out of 100, £ so far, each half.
F. REPORTING: /root/pe-builder/progress.py must write the league table summary into reports/grid_progress.md every hour. History-file results may appear ONLY as a count of ideas, never as £ and never in a ranked money table - fix the current file, which ranks history by £ a month. Change /root/pe-sports/team/friday_four.py so its third line reads from the league table: ways tested this week, ways passing, new ways by source a / b / c, and the top way's £ a month; keep it to four lines in total; back the file up first and keep it compiling.
G. RUNNING: a background loop (nohup, nice) that re-tests every way when new days of data arrive (at most every 6 hours, a plain sleep loop is fine - it is not an AI loop), registered in /root/pe-mechanic/watch.list.

HARD RULES: Paper only. Never place, cancel or change an order; never call any order endpoint; never touch anything named bookie, bot, live_, auto_bettor, or /root/REAL_MONEY_OFF. Never delete data, drop or truncate any table you did not create, or remove files; back up any file before you edit it. Do not change crontab, credentials, /root/pe-queue/runner, the recorders or the team loop. No email, no notifications, never ask Peter for anything. One heavy query at a time; statement timeouts on; work in day chunks. Leave /root/pe-sports/team/grid_history.py and run_grid_recorded.sh running.
HONESTY: recorded data is about three weeks. Most rows will not reach 300 filled orders yet: show them with their real count and 'not enough orders yet', do not hide them and do not lower the bar. If a result looks too good, find the flaw before you report it (survivors, stale quotes, look-ahead, fills that could not have happened, recorder delay) and say what you checked.

WHEN YOU STOP: write /root/pe-queue/reports/builder_handover_2.md, plain English, under 45 lines: what you built, what the tests check and that they pass, how many ways are in the table by source, how many pass, what is running, what is not done and why, what the next session should do first. Commit and push: cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/builder_handover_2.md reports/league_table.md reports/paper_forward.md reports/grid_progress.md 2>/dev/null; git commit -qm "builder session 2: league table" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q. Then end the session.
BRIEF
cat > $B/run_builder_league.sh <<'SH'
#!/bin/bash
CL=$(cat /root/pe-watchman/claude_path); ts=$(date -u +%F_%H%M)
cd /root/pe-sports/grid && timeout -k 60 21600 "$CL" -p "$(cat /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_league.md)" --allowedTools "Bash,Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep" --max-turns 600 --output-format text > /root/pe-builder/runs/${ts}_league.log 2>&1
echo "builder league session ended $(date -u '+%F %H:%M') exit $?" >> /root/pe-builder/sessions.log
/root/pe-builder/progress.sh
SH
chmod +x $B/run_builder_league.sh
{ echo "# 686 - $(date -u '+%F %H:%M') UTC"; echo '```'
echo "earlier builder sessions:"; cat $B/sessions.log 2>/dev/null | tail -3
if pgrep -f '[r]un_builder' >/dev/null; then echo "a builder session is still running - not starting a second one"; else nohup setsid $B/run_builder_league.sh > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null & sleep 40; echo "builder session 2 started: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_builder_league.sh') | $(ls -la --time-style=+%H:%M $B/runs | tail -1 | awk '{print $6, $7}')"; fi
echo "history grid running: $(pgrep -fc '[g]rid_history.py') | recorded grid loop: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_grid_recorded.sh')"
echo '```'; } > $R 2>&1
cd /root/pe-queue && git add $R; git commit -qm "686: builder session 2 - one test, one league table" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q
